# verify

## Intent
Run focused repository-owned verification without repeated heavy commands, while keeping repository-wide verification an explicit operator choice.

## Contract
Input: the `verify` tool accepts `types`, `lint`, or `test` with an optional file; `/harness-verify-all [worktree]` is the only repository-wide entrypoint.
Output: `{ ok, status, worktree, failures: [{ file, line, rule, message }], duration }`.
Errors: repository facts, package discovery, and command transport failures are structured completed results; caller cancellation is interrupted.

## Behaviour
1. Resolve an absolute file through `RepoMapService.repositoryFactsFor` and run inside the owning worktree.
2. Run only the requested focused check from the agent-facing tool.
3. Run repository-wide checks only after the operator invokes `/harness-verify-all`.
4. Serialize heavy verification plans across repositories.
5. Cache a completed plan by action, selected worktree, file, Git HEAD, and working-tree status.
6. Reuse the cached report when the selected snapshot is unchanged.
7. Run Vitest scripts with `--run` so they terminate instead of entering watch mode.
8. Preserve complete command output and parse actionable diagnostics.

## Edge cases
- A missing or relative file preserves session-checkout behavior.
- An untrusted Git fingerprint disables caching rather than reusing stale evidence.
- A failed focused command returns diagnostics but does not launch repository-wide checks.
- An explicit full verification may still report pre-existing repository failures without blocking a focused agent workflow.
- Cancellation returns an interrupted report and never enters the cache.

## Non-goals
This tool does not mutate files, create commits or merge requests, or force agents to run repository-wide checks.

## Evidence
The measured CI-6833 session ran 23 verification calls and spent more than 18 minutes in four repository-wide plans; two unchanged dashboard plans repeated the same baseline failures.
